        {
          "_id": "5c9e1964fdae3c762882d557",
          "alias": "Sapphire Bass",
          "aliasID": "65cd9740ed6eefce4c7e13d1",
          "anonymized": false,
          "avatarURL": "https://canny-assets.io/images/21f415b3bf6ef2cacb6da9c832346e76.png",
          "created": "2019-03-29T13:11:00.593Z",
          "name": "ZarathustraDK",
          "serviceAccount": false,
          "urlName": "henrik-peytz"
        }
      ],
      "mergeID": null,
      "parentID": "61a921dad475184444aa0441",
      "pinned": false,
      "postDeleted": false,
      "postID": "59ce79bc2d132f03b0618fff",
      "private": false,
      "reactions": {
        "like": 2
      },
      "sourceLink": null,
      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "@{5c9e1964fdae3c762882d557|full_name}: That is NOT the issue. If you run VRChat's provided build of yt-dlp in the wineprefix for the game, it returns URLs perfectly fine.\nThe problem is down to Windows Media Foundation support. When you build a game in Unity, it makes video players use the platform's native multimedia rendering capabilities, so on Windows that's WMF. The problem is WMF is a cesspool of proprietary code and Proton developers are still slowly figuring it out after many years.\nAs a result, developers have disabled AVPro when the game detects that the running environment is Wine/Proton. Unity native video players are still technically enabled because they just fail gracefully instead of crashing the whole game.\nYou can't make a Linux native build of the game because it would be incompatible with Windows avatar and world assets, and there would be a similar divide in content as there is with Quest. And even if you were to include DXVK-Native into the Linux build, all the bits of content that isn't shader/texture stuff would simply not work.\nThe best option for VRChat is to wait for Proton to implement working WMF video playback. (Valve, Collabora, Please.) and once that happens, non-stream Unity native players should work right away, and then the developers can start testing again if AVPro works or not and remove the Wine/Proton checks if it's successful.\nCheck the issue for VRChat on the Proton GitHub repository if you want to follow development day-to-day. https://github.com/ValveSoftware/Proton/issues/1199",
      "viewerReactions": null
    },
